      "provenance": {
        "notes": "Tuberous begonia, not wax begonia (Begonia cucullata, own row) and not the Rex or angel-wing houseplants. Seasonal pot plant: tubers are started indoors in late winter, planted out after frost and lifted and stored dry (about 7 C, RHS; 45 to 55 F, Clemson) before the first frost, so the rest factor is unknown and the winter 'care' is storage, not watering. Watering: all three pages say moist, well-drained, regular, with tuber rot if too wet; NC State also says not drought tolerant. Light: partial shade (NC State 2 to 6 hours of direct sun; Clemson and RHS agree, RHS says sun or partial shade and scorch in hot sun); mapped to bright_indirect (agent). Size: NC State 1 to 1.5 ft tall and 1 to 2 ft wide; RHS 15 to 50 cm both ways. Humidity: NC State says it dislikes heat and humidity, no figure. Toxicity: ASPCA (Begonia spp.: soluble calcium oxalates, vomiting and salivation in dogs and cats, underground parts worst) and NC State (low severity, sap irritates skin, a problem for children) agree; neither grades it beyond 'low'. Graded 1 as the agent's mapping, no page says 2. The child line has no symptoms because the pages give none specific to people."
      },
